@page "/components/themeswitcher"

<PageOutlet Url="components/themeswitcher"
            Title="ThemeSwitcher"
            Description="BitThemeSwitcher offers the design system an app is dressed in and the light/dark scheme it shows, as one control that keeps both halves of the applied theme in step." />

<DemoPage Name="ThemeSwitcher"
          SecondaryNames="@(["DesignSystem", "DarkMode", "Theming"])"
          Description="BitThemeSwitcher is the chrome for the two choices a themed app usually puts in its header: which design system it is dressed in (Fluent, Material, Cupertino) and whether that design system is showing its light or its dark scheme. Both halves resolve to one bit-theme name applied through BitThemeManager, so switching the design system keeps the current scheme and toggling the scheme keeps the current design system."
          Parameters="componentParameters"
          SubClasses="componentSubClasses"
          GitHubExtrasUrl="ThemeSwitcher/BitThemeSwitcher.razor.cs"
          GitHubDemoUrl="../Theming/ThemeSwitcher/BitThemeSwitcherDemo.razor">
    <NotesTemplate>
        <BitText>
            To use this component, you need to install the
            <BitLink Href="https://www.nuget.org/packages/Bit.BlazorUI.Extras" Target="_blank">
                <BitTag Reversed
                        Text="Bit.BlazorUI.Extras"
                        Color="BitColor.SecondaryBackground"
                        IconName="@BitIconName.NavigateExternalInline" />
            </BitLink>
            nuget package, as described in the Optional steps of the
            <BitLink Href="/getting-started">Getting started</BitLink> page.
        </BitText>
        <br />
        <BitText>
            The Material and Cupertino design systems are override-only stylesheet bundles that ship in that same
            package, so offering them only does something on a page that links the bundle after the core stylesheet
            (see the <BitLink Href="/theming#presets">Presets</BitLink> section of the Theming page). This website
            links both, which is why the switchers below actually re-skin it.
        </BitText>
    </NotesTemplate>
    <Examples>
        <DemoExample Title="Basic" RazorCode="@example1RazorCode" Id="example1">
            <div>
                The default switcher: the three packaged design systems, and the light/dark toggle. It acts on the
                document, so a pick here re-skins this whole website live - exactly like the one in the site header,
                which is this very component.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitThemeSwitcher />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Design systems" RazorCode="@example2RazorCode" Id="example2">
            <div>
                DesignSystems replaces the offered list. Value is the stem the two scheme names share, so an item
                needs LightTheme and DarkTheme spelled out only when its themes are not named "{Value}-light" and
                "{Value}-dark" - as with Fluent, whose pair the core stylesheet calls "light" and "dark". The first
                item is also the fallback the picker shows for an applied theme that no item claims.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitThemeSwitcher DesignSystems="customDesignSystems" />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Parts" RazorCode="@example3RazorCode" Id="example3">
            <div>
                NoColorScheme and NoDesignSystem drop either half, for chrome that has room for only one of them or
                that puts them in different places:
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitThemeSwitcher NoColorScheme />
            <br />
            <BitThemeSwitcher NoDesignSystem />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Icons & titles" RazorCode="@example4RazorCode" Id="example4">
            <div>
                Each control names what it does through its own title (which is also its accessible label), and the
                two toggle glyphs are Fluent UI icon names. Every one of them is a parameter:
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitThemeSwitcher DesignSystemTitle="Skin"
                              DarkSchemeTitle="Go dark"
                              LightSchemeTitle="Go light"
                              DarkSchemeIconName="@BitIconName.Brightness"
                              LightSchemeIconName="@BitIconName.Sunny" />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Events" RazorCode="@example5RazorCode" Id="example5">
            <div>
                OnChange reports the theme that was applied, whichever half of the control applied it. That is the
                hook for whatever else has to follow the theme but is not painted by the page - the site header uses
                it to repaint the native status bar on MAUI.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitThemeSwitcher OnChange="t => changedTheme = t" />
            <br />
            <div>Applied theme: @changedTheme</div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Style & Class" RazorCode="@example6RazorCode" Id="example6">
            <div>Use Style, Class, Styles and Classes to customize the switcher:</div>
            <br />
            <BitThemeSwitcher Style="padding:0.5rem;border:1px dashed gray;border-radius:0.5rem" />
            <br />
            <BitThemeSwitcher Classes="@(new() { ColorSchemeButton = "custom-scheme-btn" })" />
            <br />
            <BitThemeSwitcher Styles="@(new() { Root = "gap:1.5rem", ColorSchemeButton = "border-radius:0.25rem" })" />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="RTL" RazorCode="@example7RazorCode" Id="example7">
            <div>Use Dir="BitDir.Rtl" to change the direction of the switcher:</div>
            <br />
            <BitThemeSwitcher Dir="BitDir.Rtl" />
        </DemoExample>
    </Examples>
</DemoPage>
